    "lawCode": "Amd §§1125 & 305, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S5261A\n\nSPONSOR: SALAZAR\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to prohibiting corporal\npunishment in schools\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill clarifies that corporal punishment in an educational setting\nconstitutes child abuse.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends subdivision 1 of section 1125 of the education law to\nadd corporal punishment to the definition of \"child abuse\".\n\nSection two amends section 305 of the education law to add a new subdi-\nvision 60 directing the commissioner to promulgate rules and regulations\nprohibiting any employee or agent of any school from using corporal\npunishment.\n\nSection three is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCorporal punishment is an inhumane and counterproductive form of disci-\npline and has no place in New York's educational settings. The United\nStates is only one of three developed nations to permit corporal punish-\nment in educational settings, alongside Singapore and Australia; it is\nbanned by 128 other nations. Scientific studies have shown that students\nexposed to corporal punishment exhibit lower test scores and increased\nchances of behavioral disorders, experience more feelings of inadequacy\nand resentment, may be more aggressive and violent, and demonstrate\nreduced problem-solving abilities, social competence and academic\nachievement.\n\nThis bill would codify that corporal punishment in educational settings\nis a form of child abuse and requires the commissioner to promulgate\nrules and regulations clearly prohibiting the use of corporal punishment\nin New York in any school.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nThe \"A\" print of the bill modifies the specific provisions of the educa-\ntion law being amended, clarifies that corporal punishment is a form of\nchild abuse, and requires the commissioner to promulgate regulations.\nThis amendment makes this bill a \"same as\" to A 5010A, which passed the\nAssembly in April 2023.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill is effective immediately.\n",
